<main>
  <header>
    <h1>China Transit Visa & 240-Hour Visa-Free Transit Guide</h1>
    <p>Last verified: August 28, 2026</p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>Quick Answer</h2>

    <p>
      China's 240-hour visa-free transit policy allows eligible travelers to enter
      Mainland China without obtaining a regular Chinese visa when they are
      transiting through China to a different country or region.
    </p>

    <p>
      As of August 2026, the policy covers nationals of 57 countries, including
      the United States, and applies through 65 designated ports in 24
      provincial-level regions. Eligible travelers may remain within the
      designated permitted areas for no more than 240 hours, subject to the
      policy requirements.
    </p>

    <p><strong>The most important route rule is:</strong></p>

    <p>
      The country or region immediately before Mainland China and the country or
      region immediately after Mainland China must be different.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Los Angeles &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; Tokyo:</strong> passes the transit-route
        test because the relevant route is United States &rarr; Mainland China &rarr; Japan.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Los Angeles &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; San Francisco:</strong> does not pass the
        transit-route test because the relevant route is United States &rarr; Mainland
        China &rarr; United States.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      A route that passes this test is not automatically eligible for 240-hour
      visa-free transit. Your nationality, passport/travel document, onward
      travel, entry and exit ports, length of stay, permitted travel areas and
      other requirements must also qualify. Immigration authorities make the
      final decision during entry inspection.
    </p>
  </section>

<section>
  <h2>240-Hour Transit Eligibility Checklist</h2>

  <p>Use this checklist to see whether your trip appears to meet the core published requirements.</p>

  <h3 class="check-item">You are a national of an eligible country</h3>
  <p>
    China currently applies the 240-hour visa-free transit policy to nationals
    of 57 countries, including the United States.
  </p>

  <h3 class="check-item">Your route is a true international transit</h3>
  <p>
    You must travel Country/Region A &rarr; Mainland China &rarr; Country/Region B,
    with A and B different. For this test, use the country or region immediately
    before Mainland China and the one immediately after it. The detailed route
    examples in Sections 3 and 4 show how layovers affect this rule.
  </p>

  <h3 class="check-item">You enter and leave through eligible ports</h3>
  <p>
    The policy currently applies through 65 designated ports across 24
    provincial-level regions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not every international airport, railway station, seaport or land crossing
    automatically qualifies, so you should verify both your entry and exit
    arrangements against the current official list.
  </p>

  <h3 class="check-item">You leave within the permitted 240-hour period</h3>
  <p>
    For travelers admitted under the policy, the visa-free stay period is
    calculated from 00:00 on the day following the day of entry.
  </p>

  <h3 class="check-item">You remain within the permitted travel areas</h3>
  <p>
    Travel under the 240-hour policy is limited to the designated permitted
    areas associated with the policy. You should verify that every place you
    plan to visit in Mainland China falls within the currently permitted areas.
  </p>

  <h3 class="check-item">You have the required travel documents</h3>
  <p>Current National Immigration Administration guidance states that applicants must:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>hold a valid international travel document with at least three months of validity;</li>
    <li>meet the entry requirements for the onward country or region;</li>
    <li>hold confirmed onward travel to a third country or region within the permitted period, or other qualifying proof;</li>
    <li>complete the required arrival-card information; and</li>
    <li>cooperate with immigration inspection.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Important:</strong> Meeting the published requirements does not
    guarantee entry. Final approval is subject to inspection by Chinese
    immigration authorities.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Country A &rarr; Mainland China &rarr; Country B: The Route Rule</h2>

  <p>
    One of the most confusing parts of the 240-hour policy is the requirement
    that the traveler be in transit to a third country or region.
  </p>

  <p>
    The simplest way to evaluate the route is to ignore most of the itinerary
    and look at the two places immediately surrounding Mainland China:
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Country/Region A &rarr; Mainland China &rarr; Country/Region B</strong>
  </p>

  <p>
    For the route requirement to work, A cannot be the same country or region as B.
  </p>

  <h3>Your overall trip can start and end in the same country</h3>
  <p>
    Your entire vacation may begin and end in the same country. For the 240-hour
    route test, only the country or region immediately before Mainland China and
    the country or region immediately after Mainland China matter.
  </p>

  <h3>Layovers matter</h3>
  <p>
    A layover immediately before or after Mainland China changes which country
    or region counts as A or B. Evaluate the actual international sequence
    around Mainland China rather than only the origin and final destination of
    the full trip. See Section 4 for route examples.
  </p>

  <h3>Different airports in the same country do not make them different countries</h3>
  <p>
    For example, Los Angeles &rarr; Mainland China &rarr; San Francisco is still
    United States &rarr; Mainland China &rarr; United States.
  </p>
  <p>
    Los Angeles and San Francisco are different cities and airports, but they
    are both in the United States. The route therefore does not meet the
    third-country transit requirement.
  </p>

  <h3>Hong Kong, Macao and Taiwan</h3>
  <p>
    For purposes of the visa-free transit policy, official Chinese government
    guidance identifies Hong Kong SAR, Macao SAR and Taiwan as third
    countries/regions for the transit requirement.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, Hong Kong &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; Los Angeles has the relevant
    route Hong Kong &rarr; Mainland China &rarr; United States and can therefore
    pass the route-structure test, assuming the other policy requirements are met.
  </p>
</section>

