  <p>
    A route-test PASS is only one condition. You must also meet the nationality,
    port, timing, permitted-area, travel-document and onward-travel requirements
    described in this guide.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Eligible Countries and Travelers</h2>

  <p>
    The 240-hour visa-free transit policy currently applies to nationals of
    57 countries.
  </p>

  <h3>Eligible countries</h3>

  <h4>Europe - 40 countries</h4>
  <p>
    Austria, Belgium, Czech Republic, Denmark, Estonia, Finland, France,
    Germany, Greece, Hungary, Iceland, Italy, Latvia, Lithuania, Luxembourg,
    Malta, the Netherlands, Poland, Portugal, Slovakia, Slovenia, Spain,
    Sweden, Switzerland, Monaco, Russia, the United Kingdom, Ireland, Cyprus,
    Bulgaria, Romania, Ukraine, Serbia, Croatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina,
    Montenegro, North Macedonia, Albania, Belarus, and Norway.
  </p>

  <h4>Americas - 6 countries</h4>
  <p>
    United States, Canada, Brazil, Mexico, Argentina, and Chile.
  </p>

  <h4>Oceania - 2 countries</h4>
  <p>
    Australia and New Zealand.
  </p>

  <h4>Asia - 9 countries</h4>
  <p>
    South Korea, Japan, Singapore, Brunei, United Arab Emirates, Qatar,
    Indonesia, Kyrgyzstan, and Vietnam.
  </p>

  <p>
    The list most recently expanded on August 20, 2026, when Kyrgyzstan and
    Vietnam were added, bringing the total from 55 to 57 countries.
  </p>

  <h3>Your nationality matters, not where you live or where your trip begins</h3>
  <p>
    Eligibility for this part of the policy is based on your
    nationality/travel document.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a U.S. citizen living in the United Kingdom does not need to
    begin the trip in the United States. The route itself is evaluated
    separately under the Country/Region A &rarr; Mainland China &rarr;
    Country/Region B rule explained above.
  </p>

  <h3>Is there an age restriction?</h3>
  <p>
    No age restriction is stated under the current policy. Children may use the
    240-hour visa-free transit policy if they meet the same applicable
    requirements, including nationality, travel-document, itinerary and entry
    conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    For passport validity and U.S.-specific passport guidance, see the Document
    Checklist in Section 8.
  </p>

  <h3>What if your nationality is not on the list?</h3>
  <p>
    You cannot rely on the 240-hour policy solely on the basis of transit if
    your nationality is not one of the 57 currently covered countries.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, another Chinese visa-free policy may apply depending on your
    circumstances, such as:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>the 24-hour visa-free transit policy;</li>
    <li>a unilateral or mutual visa exemption;</li>
    <li>or the separate Hainan visa-free entry policy.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>We compare these policies later in this guide.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How the 240 Hours Are Calculated</h2>

  <p>
    The 240-hour period does not normally begin at the moment your flight lands.
  </p>

  <p>
    Under current National Immigration Administration guidance, the visa-free
    stay period is calculated from 00:00 on the day following the day of entry.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose you enter Mainland China on June 1 at 3:00 PM.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your 240-hour clock begins at June 2 at 00:00.
  </p>

  <p>
    240 hours equals 10 full 24-hour days, so the 240-hour period runs until
    June 12 at 00:00.
  </p>

  <p>
    In practical terms, the ten counted days are June 2 through June 11, and
    you would need to leave within the period authorized by immigration.
  </p>

  <h3>Why can your actual time in China be longer than exactly 240 elapsed hours?</h3>

  <p>
    Because the official 240-hour calculation begins at 00:00 on the day after
    entry, the time between your admission on the arrival day and midnight is
    not part of the 240-hour count.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if you enter at 9:00 AM, there may be almost 15 hours before
    the 240-hour clock begins.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, you should not plan your departure solely from your own
    calculation. The immigration authority will complete the temporary-entry
    formalities and indicate your authorized stay. Follow the period authorized
    by immigration, particularly if it differs from what you expected.
  </p>

  <h3>Does the departure flight have to leave within the 240-hour period?</h3>

  <p>
    Yes. Your confirmed onward travel must be scheduled within the permitted
    transit period. Current NIA requirements call for onward travel to a third
    country or region with a confirmed date and arrangement within 240 hours,
    or other qualifying proof.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Important:</strong> Flight delays, cancellations and other
    emergencies can create an overstay risk. We explain what to do if you
    cannot leave before your authorized stay expires later in this guide.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Eligible Ports and Permitted Stay Areas</h2>

  <p>
    The 240-hour policy does not apply at every possible border crossing or
    allow unrestricted travel throughout Mainland China.
  </p>

  <p>
    There are currently 65 designated entry and exit ports across 24
    provincial-level regions.
  </p>

  <h3>The 24 participating regions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Beijing</li>
    <li>Tianjin</li>
    <li>Hebei</li>
    <li>Liaoning</li>
    <li>Heilongjiang</li>
    <li>Shanghai</li>
    <li>Jiangsu</li>
    <li>Zhejiang</li>
    <li>Fujian</li>
    <li>Shandong</li>
    <li>Henan</li>
    <li>Hubei</li>
    <li>Hunan</li>
    <li>Guangdong</li>
    <li>Guangxi</li>
    <li>Chongqing</li>
    <li>Sichuan</li>
    <li>Yunnan</li>
    <li>Shaanxi</li>
    <li>Shanxi</li>
    <li>Anhui</li>
    <li>Jiangxi</li>
    <li>Hainan</li>
    <li>Guizhou</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Travelers may make cross-provincial trips within the designated permitted
    areas rather than being restricted to the city where they entered.
  </p>

  <h3>Important: "24 regions" does not mean every place within all 24 regions is automatically permitted</h3>

  <p>
    The permitted geography differs by region. Some participating provinces
    permit travel throughout the entire province, while others cover only
    particular cities or administrative areas.
  </p>

  <p>For example, NIA guidance states that after the 240-hour expansion:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Anhui, Hainan and Guizhou allow travel throughout the province;</li>
    <li>Fujian, Hubei and Shaanxi were expanded to the whole province;</li>
    <li>Shanxi's permitted area includes Taiyuan and Datong;</li>
    <li>Jiangxi's permitted area includes Nanchang and Jingdezhen;</li>
    <li>Guangxi covers a specified group of cities rather than automatically every location in the autonomous region.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Therefore, do not simply ask "Is this province covered?" Instead ask:
    "Is the specific city or area I plan to visit within the currently permitted area?"
  </p>

  <h3>Your entry and exit ports must also be eligible</h3>

  <p>
    The 65 participating ports include different types of border crossings, not
    only airports.
  </p>

  <p>Depending on the location, eligible ports include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>international airports;</li>
    <li>passenger seaports;</li>
    <li>certain railway ports;</li>
    <li>and certain land-border crossings.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For example, the policy now includes West Kowloon Station of the
    Guangzhou-Shenzhen-Hong Kong Express Rail Link, following the addition of
    five Guangdong ports in November 2025.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is important because older online guides may still say that travelers
    cannot enter Mainland China from Hong Kong by high-speed rail under the policy.
  </p>

  <h3>Can you enter through one eligible port and leave through another?</h3>

  <p>
    Yes, provided your itinerary continues to satisfy the policy requirements
    and both ports are applicable to the 240-hour program.
  </p>

  <p>
    Official guidance states that qualifying travelers may travel within
    designated areas across participating regions and depart through an
    applicable port under the policy.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a traveler may not necessarily have to use Shanghai Pudong
    Airport for both entry and exit. A route involving a different eligible
    departure port may be possible, provided all other route, geographic and
    timing requirements are met.
  </p>

  <h3>Check the eligible ports and permitted stay areas</h3>

  <p>
    Because the permitted area depends on where you enter and travel, you should
    check your planned entry port, exit port, and every Mainland China
    destination before relying on the 240-hour policy.
  </p>

  <p>
    China's National Immigration Administration publishes the official list of
    the 65 eligible ports and their permitted stay areas.
  </p>

  <PortTable />

  <p>
    <a href="https://en.nia.gov.cn/n147413/c187308/content.html">
      See the official NIA list of eligible ports and permitted stay areas
    </a>
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Document Checklist</h2>

  <p>
    It is helpful to separate published eligibility requirements from documents
    that are simply useful to carry.
  </p>

  <h3>Documents required under the published 240-hour policy</h3>

  <p>
    Current National Immigration Administration guidance requires the following
    core items.
  </p>

  <h4>1. A valid international travel document</h4>

  <p>
    Your travel document must generally have at least three months of remaining
    validity for purposes of the 240-hour policy.
  </p>

  <p>
    As noted above, travelers should also check any stricter passport
    requirements that apply to their nationality or onward destination.
  </p>

  <h4>2. Proof that you can enter your next country or region</h4>

  <p>
    You must satisfy the entry requirements of the country or region you will
    travel to after Mainland China.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if your onward destination requires you to have a visa, an
    electronic travel authorization, or another entry document, you should have
    that authorization before relying on the China transit policy.
  </p>

  <h4>3. Confirmed onward travel to a third country or region</h4>

  <p>
    You need confirmed onward travel within the permitted period.
  </p>

  <p>
    NIA guidance refers to an onward ticket with a confirmed date and
    arrangement to a third country or region within 240 hours, or other
    qualifying proof documents.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your onward itinerary therefore needs to demonstrate both when you will
    leave Mainland China and where you are going next.
  </p>

  <h4>4. Arrival Card information</h4>

  <p>
    Travelers must provide the required arrival information.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since November 20, 2025, foreigners can complete China's Arrival Card
    information online before traveling through official NIA channels.
  </p>

  <p>
    Travelers who do not complete it in advance can generally complete the
    information after arriving through an online system, smart devices or a
    paper Arrival Card at the port.
  </p>

  <h4>5. Immigration inspection</h4>

  <p>
    You must cooperate with the immigration inspection authority at your port
    of entry.
  </p>

  <p>
    Meeting the document requirements does not automatically grant admission.
    Immigration authorities review the application and complete the
    temporary-entry formalities after arrival.
  </p>

  <h3>Documents we recommend carrying even when they are not listed as core national eligibility requirements</h3>

  <p>
    The following are useful because airline staff or immigration officers may
    want to understand your trip quickly:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>accommodation reservations or the address where you will stay;</li>
    <li>a simple copy of your China itinerary;</li>
    <li>offline or printed copies of inbound and onward tickets;</li>
    <li>evidence of onward-destination entry authorization where applicable;</li>
    <li>contact information for your hotel or host;</li>
    <li>a copy or saved link to the current official 240-hour policy.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Practical recommendation ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â not presented as a universal legal requirement:
    keeping the key documents available offline can help if you do not yet have
    mobile data after landing.
  </p>

  <h3>Do you need a hotel reservation to qualify?</h3>

  <p>
    A hotel reservation is not listed among the core national eligibility
    conditions in the NIA requirements we reviewed. The published conditions
    focus on nationality, travel-document validity, onward-destination
    eligibility, confirmed onward travel, Arrival Card information and
    immigration inspection.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, you may need accommodation details for arrival formalities, and
    accommodation-registration rules apply after entry. We cover those
    requirements later in this guide.
  </p>

  <h3>U.S. passport holders: use the stricter passport guidance</h3>

  <p>For U.S. citizens, Travel.State.gov currently states that the passport should:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>be valid for at least six months beyond the date of arrival;</li>
    <li>have at least two blank pages;</li>
    <li>and not be the 12-page U.S. emergency passport when seeking visa-free entry.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This is stricter than the three-month validity condition stated in NIA's
    240-hour eligibility rules. For a U.S.-focused traveler guide, we recommend
    planning around the six-month requirement rather than relying on the lower
    three-month policy threshold.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Airline Check-In and Chinese Immigration</h2>

  <p>
    Using the 240-hour visa-free transit policy involves two separate stages:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>getting accepted for your journey by your airline or other international carrier; and</li>
    <li>receiving approval for temporary entry from Chinese immigration after you arrive.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>These are not the same decision.</p>

  <h3>Before boarding your flight to China</h3>

  <p>
    Keep the core documents from Section 8 easy to access, especially your
    passport, confirmed onward travel and any required authorization for your
    next destination. Airline procedures vary, so be prepared to explain that
    you plan to use China's 240-hour visa-free transit policy and show your
    onward itinerary. Official Beijing guidance advises travelers to inform the
    airline when boarding.
  </p>

  <p>
    Practical tip: Keep your onward ticket and key documents available offline
    or in print.
  </p>

  <h3>Do you need to apply at a Chinese embassy before traveling?</h3>

  <p>
    No separate embassy application is required for the 240-hour visa-free
    transit permit itself.
  </p>

  <p>
    Current official Shanghai guidance states that eligible travelers can apply
    upon arrival at the Chinese port of entry and do not need to contact a
    Chinese embassy in advance. Final approval remains subject to on-site
    immigration review.
  </p>

  <p>
    You may complete your Arrival Card information in advance, as explained in
    Section 8, but this is different from obtaining advance approval for
    240-hour visa-free transit.
  </p>

  <h3>What happens after you arrive in China?</h3>

  <p>
    The exact layout and procedure can vary by port, but the general process is:
  </p>

  <h4>1. Follow signs for 24/240-hour visa-free transit or temporary entry permits</h4>

  <p>
    Major participating ports may have designated counters or special channels
    for travelers using the policy. Shanghai, for example, provides special
    channels for 240-hour visa-free transit at its participating ports.
  </p>

  <h4>2. Present your documents</h4>

  <p>
    Present the core documents listed in Section 8 and any additional
    information requested by the immigration officer.
  </p>

  <h4>3. Undergo immigration inspection</h4>

  <p>
    The immigration authority will review your itinerary and other requirements.
  </p>

  <p>
    If your application is approved, you will receive authorization for
    temporary entry under the 240-hour visa-free transit policy. Official
    Shanghai guidance states that a temporary entry permit sticker may be placed
    in the passport and can then be used for purposes such as hotel check-in and
    accommodation registration.
  </p>

  <h4>4. Continue through the normal arrival process</h4>

  <p>
    After immigration approval, travelers can proceed with baggage collection,
    customs procedures and entry into China according to the arrangements at
    that port. Official Beijing guidance describes the sequence as
    temporary-entry processing followed by baggage collection and customs.
  </p>

  <h3>Does meeting all the published requirements guarantee entry?</h3>

  <p>No.</p>

  <p>
    Even when your nationality, route, ports, timing and documents appear to
    satisfy the published rules, the final decision is made through on-site
    immigration inspection.
  </p>

  <p>
    For that reason, throughout this guide we use language such as "Your
    itinerary appears to meet the published requirements" rather than "Your
    entry is guaranteed."
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Activities Allowed During the 240-Hour Stay</h2>

  <p>
    Travelers admitted under the 240-hour visa-free transit policy may engage
    in a range of short-term activities.
  </p>

  <p>
    Current National Immigration Administration guidance specifically includes:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>tourism;</li>
    <li>business;</li>
    <li>visits;</li>
    <li>family visits;</li>
    <li>and other permitted short-term activities.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This means the policy can be used for more than simply remaining near an
    airport during a connection.
  </p>

  <p>
    Subject to the geographic rules explained in Section 7, eligible travelers
    may visit destinations within the designated permitted areas during their
    authorized stay.
  </p>

  <h3>What activities are not covered?</h3>

  <p>
    Activities that require prior government approval still require the
    appropriate visa or authorization before entering China.
  </p>

  <p>Current NIA guidance specifically identifies:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>work;</li>
    <li>study;</li>
    <li>news reporting;</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    as activities that require the appropriate prior approval and visa.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the purpose of your trip falls into one of these categories, do not
    assume that the 240-hour visa-free transit policy allows you to carry out
    that activity simply because your itinerary otherwise qualifies.
  </p>

  <h3>What about remote work?</h3>

  <p>
    Current official guidance clearly distinguishes permitted short-term
    business activities from work that requires prior approval, but the
    official sources we reviewed do not clearly define every remote-work
    scenario.
  </p>

  <p>
    If your planned activity could be considered employment or work in China
    rather than ordinary business travel, confirm the requirements with the
    National Immigration Administration or the appropriate Chinese authority
    before relying on the 240-hour policy.
  </p>

  <h3>Stay within the permitted area and authorized time</h3>

  <p>
    Using the 240-hour policy does not give unrestricted permission to travel
    anywhere in Mainland China.
  </p>

  <p>During your stay, you must:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>remain within the designated permitted areas;</li>
    <li>leave through an eligible exit port;</li>
    <li>and depart within the period authorized by immigration.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Remember accommodation registration</h3>

  <p>
    Foreign travelers are also subject to China's accommodation-registration
    requirements.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you stay in a hotel, the hotel is responsible for registering your
    accommodation with the local public security authority.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you stay somewhere other than a hotel, such as a private home, you or
    the person accommodating you must generally complete accommodation
    registration with the local public security authority within 24 hours of
    arrival at that accommodation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Starting in March 2026, China began piloting online accommodation
    registration for non-hotel stays in several regions, while in-person
    registration remains available.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are unsure how to register where you are staying, ask your host,
    contact the local public security authority, or call the NIA immigration
    service hotline 12367.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>If You Cannot Leave Before Your Authorized Stay Expires</h2>

  <p>
    Do not simply remain in China beyond the period authorized under your
    temporary entry permit.
  </p>

  <p>
    If an emergency, sudden illness, force majeure or another legitimate
    circumstance means that you may be unable to leave on time, you should
    contact Chinese immigration authorities before your authorized stay expires.
  </p>

  <p>
    Official Chinese guidance provides a process for visa-free entrants who
    legitimately need to remain beyond their authorized visa-free period to
    apply for an appropriate stay permit.
  </p>

  <h3>Where do you apply?</h3>

  <p>
    Applications are handled by the Exit-Entry Administration of the local
    Public Security Bureau.
  </p>

  <p>
    NIA's national service guide states that stay-permit applications are
    accepted by authorized exit-entry administration authorities, generally at
    the prefecture-level city level or above, with some authorized county-level
    offices also able to handle applications.
  </p>

  <p>You normally need to apply in person.</p>

  <h3>How do you find the correct Exit-Entry Administration office?</h3>

  <p>
    If you do not know which office serves your location, a practical starting
    point is the China Immigration Service Hotline 12367.
  </p>

  <p>
    From outside China, the hotline can be reached at +86-12367. It operates
    around the clock and provides English-language service, along with several
    other language options.
  </p>

  <p>
    The hotline can help direct you to the appropriate local immigration or
    exit-entry authority.
  </p>

  <p>
    Major city government websites may also publish local Exit-Entry
    Administration office addresses and opening hours.
  </p>

  <h3>What documents may be required?</h3>

  <p>The NIA stay-permit service guide lists the basic application materials as:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>a valid passport or other international travel document;</li>
    <li>a Visa/Stay Permit/Residence Permit Application Form;</li>
    <li>a compliant photograph;</li>
    <li>documents supporting the reason why the additional stay is necessary;</li>
    <li>and any other materials required by the authority handling the application.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The exact supporting documents will depend on why you cannot leave.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a flight cancellation, medical emergency or other unexpected
    event may require evidence showing what happened.
  </p>

  <h3>Do not wait until the last minute</h3>

  <p>
    A delayed or canceled flight does not by itself mean that your authorized
    stay has automatically been extended.
  </p>

  <p>
    If it appears that you may not be able to depart before your authorized stay
    ends, contact 12367 or the local Exit-Entry Administration as soon as
    possible and follow their instructions.
  </p>

  <p>
    NIA's general stay-permit service guide provides for a decision period of
    up to seven working days after an application is accepted, so travelers
    should not assume that an extension can always be handled immediately.
  </p>

  <h3>Is an extension guaranteed?</h3>

  <p>No.</p>

  <p>
    The authority will review the reason for the requested additional stay and
    the supporting documents before deciding whether to issue an appropriate
    stay permit.
  </p>

  <p>
    The safest rule is: If circumstances change, contact immigration authorities
    before your authorized stay expires rather than trying to resolve an
    overstay afterward.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>24-Hour Transit vs. 240-Hour Transit vs. Hainan Visa-Free Entry</h2>

  <p>
    China has several visa-free entry and transit policies, and they are not
    interchangeable. The right policy depends on your nationality, route,
    length of stay, entry and exit points, and where you plan to travel in China.
  </p>

  <h3>24-hour visa-free transit</h3>

  <p>
    China's 24-hour visa-free transit policy is available to nationals of all
    countries at China's open exit-entry ports when they are transiting to a
    third country or region within 24 hours, hold valid international travel
    documents and confirmed onward international flight, ship or train tickets,
    and meet the other applicable requirements.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you remain within the restricted area of the port, you generally do not
    need a temporary entry permit. If you need to leave the restricted port
    area, you must first obtain a temporary entry permit from the immigration
    inspection authority at that port.
  </p>

  <p>
    This policy is most relevant to travelers making a short international
    connection rather than a multi-day China stopover.
  </p>

  <h3>240-hour visa-free transit</h3>

  <p>
    The 240-hour policy covered in this guide allows a longer international
    transit stay. It currently applies to nationals of 57 eligible countries
    through 65 designated ports in 24 provincial-level regions.
  </p>

  <p>
    You must be transiting Mainland China between different countries or
    regions, and you must remain within the permitted areas and leave within
    the authorized period.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is usually the most relevant policy if you want to turn an eligible
    international transit into a China stopover of several days.
  </p>

  <h3>Hainan 30-day visa-free entry</h3>

  <p>
    Hainan has a separate regional visa-free entry policy. As of August 20,
    2026, ordinary-passport holders from 61 eligible countries, including the
    United States, may enter Hainan through its open ports and stay within
    Hainan Province for up to 30 days without a visa.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike the 240-hour policy, the Hainan policy is not based on the
    Country/Region A &rarr; Mainland China &rarr; Country/Region B transit
    structure. It is a regional visa-free entry program for eligible visits
    limited to Hainan.
  </p>

  <p>
    Official NIA guidance lists eligible short-term purposes including tourism,
    business, visits, family visits, medical treatment, conferences and
    exhibitions, and sports competitions. It does not cover work or study.
  </p>

  <h3>What if more than one visa-free policy could apply?</h3>

  <p>
    Some travelers may qualify under more than one Chinese visa-free
    arrangement, including mutual or unilateral visa exemptions. NIA guidance
    states that where a traveler is covered by a mutual or unilateral
    visa-exemption policy, the relevant provisions of that policy apply.
  </p>

  <p>
    This guide focuses on the 240-hour visa-free transit policy. If your trip is
    a short connection of less than 24 hours or a visit limited to Hainan,
    check the rules for those policies separately before traveling.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>What Happened to the Old 144-Hour Visa-Free Transit Policy?</h2>

  <p>
    If you searched for China transit information before or still see older
    pages in Google, you may encounter references to a 72-hour or 144-hour
    visa-free transit policy.
  </p>

  <p>
    China implemented the expansion from the previous 72-hour and 144-hour
    visa-free transit framework to 240 hours, or 10 days, on December 17, 2024.
    At the same time, 21 additional ports were added, bringing the then-current
    total to 60 ports across 24 provincial-level regions.
  </p>

  <p>The policy has continued to expand since then:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      November 2025: five additional Guangdong ports were added, increasing the
      number of eligible ports from 60 to 65.
    </li>
    <li>
      August 20, 2026: Kyrgyzstan and Vietnam were added to the eligible
      nationality list, increasing the number of countries covered by the
      240-hour policy from 55 to 57.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Therefore, an older page that still describes a 72-hour or 144-hour limit,
    54 or 55 eligible countries, or only 60 eligible ports may be outdated even
    if the website itself is authoritative.
  </p>

  <p>
    The separate 24-hour visa-free transit policy still exists. The change from
    72/144 hours to 240 hours did not eliminate the 24-hour transit policy.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <p>
    These answers cover common traveler edge cases. Where official guidance is
    incomplete or depends on individual circumstances, we say so explicitly.
  </p>

  <details>
    <summary>Can I use different airlines or separately booked tickets?</summary>
    <p>Official guidance is incomplete for separately booked tickets.</p>
    <p>
      Different airlines are verified. In a January 2026 Beijing government FAQ,
      a traveler asked about a Busan &rarr; Beijing &rarr; Hong Kong itinerary
      using two different airlines. The official answer states that using
      different airlines does not affect eligibility for the 240-hour visa-free
      transit policy.
    </p>
    <p>
      Separately booked tickets or separate booking references are less explicit
      in the national guidance. NIA requires confirmed onward travel to a third
      country or region within the permitted period, or other qualifying proof,
      but the sources we reviewed do not state that every possible combination of
      separately purchased tickets will be accepted.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your inbound and outbound travel is booked separately, carry clear
      confirmations for both journeys, make sure the country or region immediately
      before and after Mainland China is different, and contact NIA 12367 before
      travel if your itinerary is unusual.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do I need to apply for 240-hour visa-free transit in advance?</summary>
    <p>
      No separate advance application at a Chinese embassy or consulate is
      required for the 240-hour visa-free transit permission itself. Eligible
      travelers complete the temporary-entry process at the participating port
      after arrival.
    </p>
    <p>
      Completing the Arrival Card online before the trip is not the same thing as
      obtaining advance approval for visa-free transit. Final eligibility is still
      assessed during immigration inspection at the port of entry.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do Hong Kong, Macao and Taiwan count as a third country or region?</summary>
    <p>
      Yes. Official Chinese government guidance treats Hong Kong SAR, Macao SAR
      and Taiwan as third countries/regions for purposes of the transit-route
      requirement.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, Hong Kong &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; United States can pass the
      route-structure test because Hong Kong and the United States are different
      regions/countries for this purpose. The traveler must still satisfy the
      other nationality, port, timing, document and permitted-area requirements.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Can I enter through one eligible port and leave through another?</summary>
    <p>
      Yes. Official guidance states that after approval under the 240-hour policy,
      travelers may move within the designated permitted areas and depart through
      any applicable port under the policy.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both the entry and exit arrangements must be compatible with the current
      policy, and your travel inside Mainland China must remain within the
      designated permitted areas.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Can I use rail, ferry or land crossings instead of flying?</summary>
    <p>
      Yes, but only through designated ports covered by the 240-hour policy. The
      current 65-port list includes airports as well as passenger seaports,
      railway ports and land/road crossings.
    </p>
    <p>
      Examples include West Kowloon Station of the Guangzhou-Shenzhen-Hong Kong
      Express Rail Link, Guangzhou Pazhou Ferry Terminal, Hengqin Port and the
      Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macao Bridge Port.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not assume that every railway station, ferry terminal or land border is
      eligible. Check the current NIA port list before booking.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do I need to register my accommodation?</summary>
    <p>
      Yes. If you stay in a hotel, the hotel is responsible for submitting your
      accommodation registration information. If you stay somewhere other than a
      hotel, you or the person accommodating you must generally complete
      registration with the local public security authority within 24 hours after
      arrival at that accommodation.
    </p>
    <p>
      China began piloting online registration for non-hotel stays in specified
      regions in March 2026. Because online availability may differ by location,
      ask your host or local authorities, or contact NIA 12367 if you are unsure
      how to register.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Can I use the 240-hour policy more than once?</summary>
    <p>
      Yes. A Beijing government response citing the Exit-Entry Administration
      Department of the Beijing Municipal Public Security Bureau states that the
      240-hour visa-free transit policy has no time-interval restriction between
      qualifying entries.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each entry must independently satisfy the policy requirements, and the
      traveler must complete the temporary-entry formalities for each entry.
      Final approval remains subject to immigration inspection each time.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What if I already have a valid Chinese visa?</summary>
    <p>
      Official Shanghai Border Inspection guidance says that if a traveler both
      holds a valid Chinese visa and qualifies for the 240-hour visa-free transit
      policy, border inspection will give priority to the 240-hour visa-free
      transit process.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the traveler needs to stay longer than 240 hours, the border inspection
      authority can instead handle entry formalities using the valid Chinese visa
      according to the traveler's request.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Can prior Chinese visa refusals or immigration violations affect eligibility?</summary>
    <p>Individual cases require review.</p>
    <p>
      Current Shanghai guidance states that certain Chinese visa-refusal history
      and records of illegal entry, exit, residence or employment within the
      previous five years can affect eligibility for 240-hour visa-free transit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Older NIA temporary-entry guidance also refers to serious
      accommodation-registration violations within the previous two years.
      However, current official summaries we reviewed are not fully consistent
      about how that older condition applies to today's 240-hour policy.
    </p>
    <p>
      A visa refusal by a third country is different. In an August 2026 FAQ,
      Shanghai Border Inspection confirmed that an Australian visa refusal did
      not prevent an Indonesian traveler from applying for China's 240-hour
      visa-free transit.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you have relevant Chinese visa-refusal or immigration-violation history,
      confirm your situation with NIA 12367 before relying on visa-free transit.
    </p>
  </details>
</section>

