      framework was expanded to 240 hours, and 21 ports were added, bringing
      the total at that time to 60 ports in 24 provincial-level regions.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why the "last verified" date matters</h3>

  <p>
    Older official, airline and travel-industry pages may remain online after
    the policy changes. For current counts, ports and eligibility rules, use
    the newest applicable NIA source rather than relying only on the
    publication authority of an older page.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you find an official update that appears newer than the information in
    this guide, verify it against NIA or contact 12367 before relying on our
    summary.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Before You Travel: Final Checks</h2>

  <p>
    Before relying on the 240-hour visa-free transit policy, recheck the details
    of your trip against the latest official guidance. Policies, eligible ports
    and permitted areas can change.
  </p>

  <p>Before departure, confirm:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      the country or region immediately before and after Mainland China are different;
    </li>
    <li>
      your nationality is currently eligible for the 240-hour policy;
    </li>
    <li>
      your entry and exit ports are on the current eligible-port list;
    </li>
    <li>
      every Mainland China destination you plan to visit is within the permitted stay area;
    </li>
    <li>
      your onward travel is confirmed and scheduled within the authorized period; and
    </li>
    <li>
      your passport or travel document and onward-destination entry documents meet
      the applicable requirements.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Use the port and permitted-area table in this guide as a planning aid, and
    check the current NIA source before departure.
  </p>

  <p>
    If your itinerary involves an unusual booking structure, prior Chinese
    immigration issues, or another situation not clearly covered here, contact
    NIA 12367 for official guidance.
  </p>

  <p>
    This guide is informational and cannot guarantee admission. Chinese
    immigration authorities make the final decision at the port of entry.
  </p>
</section>

